{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-83890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $P(t)$ of owls in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.1P-0.000\\,2{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. If there were $100$ owls in the forest initially, how many owls inhabited the forest after $10$ years? Round you answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-83890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.1P-0.000\\,2{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,2P(500-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-(500\\cdot 0.000\\,2)t}}=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-0.1t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =100 \\\\ \\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-0.1(0)}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =5 \\\\ C & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+4{e}^{-0.1t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=10$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =\\frac{500}{1+4{e}^{-0.1(10)}} \\\\  & ≈202.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $10$ years, approximately $202$ owls inhabited the forest." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-83890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aad293e3b2a4ca071affa395396afe4bd71a9ae6202b3040e3761e240464603"} {"question" "q-83890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-83890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "173"] ["math" "202"] ["math" "214"] ["math" "289"] ["math" "366"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-83890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "202"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-83892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The rate of growth in the number of students $P(t)$ in a school who have heard a certain rumor follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=P-0.000\\,5{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in hours, since the rumor started spreading. If there were $2$ students who knew the rumor initially, how many students have heard the rumor after $7$ hours?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-83892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =P-0.000\\,5{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,5P(2\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=2\\,000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(2\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,5)t}}=\\frac{2\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=2$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{2\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(0)}} & =2 \\\\ 1+C & =1\\,000 \\\\ C & =999\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2\\,000}{1+999{e}^{-t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & =\\frac{2\\,000}{1+999{e}^{-7}} \\\\  & ≈1047.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $7$ hours, approximately $1047$ students have heard the rumor." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-83892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aad293e3b2a4ca071affa395396afe4bd71a9ae6202b3040e3761e240464603"} {"question" "q-83892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-83892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1047"] ["math" "1258"] ["math" "1521"] ["math" "764"] ["math" "997"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-83892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1047"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the number of students enrolled in an after-school program, $P(t),$ is jointly proportional to the number of students enrolled at time $t,$ measured in weeks, and the difference between the program’s maximum enrollment and the number of students enrolled.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.004$ and the maximum enrollment is $500,$ find an expression for the number of students enrolled after $t$ weeks, given that there were $50$ students enrolled initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $500$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the number of students enrolled grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of students enrolled at time $t$ and the difference between the maximum enrollment and the number of students enrolled, the number of students enrolled follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.004,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.004\\,P(500-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-(500\\cdot 0.004)t}}=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=50$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-2\\cdot 0}} & =50 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{500}{50} \\\\ C & =10-1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+9{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aad293e3b2a4ca071affa395396afe4bd71a9ae6202b3040e3761e240464603"} {"question" "q-353254" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353254" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9e^{-2t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the number of trees in a reforestation area, $P(t),$ is jointly proportional to the number of trees at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the area’s carrying capacity and the number of trees.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $\\frac{1}{3000}$ and the carrying capacity is $1500,$ find an expression for the number of trees after $t$ years, given that there were $300$ trees in the area initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $1500$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the number of trees grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of trees at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the number of trees, the number of trees follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{1}{3000},$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1500.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=\\frac{1}{3000}\\,P(1500-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-(1500\\cdot \\frac{1}{3000})t}}=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-t/2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=300$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0}} & =300 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1500}{300} \\\\ C & =5-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+4{e}^{-t/2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aad293e3b2a4ca071affa395396afe4bd71a9ae6202b3040e3761e240464603"} {"question" "q-353256" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353256" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4e^{-0.5t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $P(t)$ of customers signed up for a grocery store’s rewards program follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.35P-0.0007{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in months. If there were $120$ customers signed up initially, how many customers were signed up after $7$ months? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $7$ months, approximately\n{{field-1}} customers were signed up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.35P-0.0007{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0007P(500-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0007,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-(500\\cdot 0.0007)t}}=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-0.35t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=120$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-0.35(0)}} & =120 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{500}{120} \\\\ C & =\\frac{25}{6}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+\\frac{19}{6}{e}^{-0.35t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7) & =\\frac{500}{1+\\frac{19}{6}{e}^{-0.35(7)}} \\\\  & ≈393.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $7$ months, approximately $393$ customers were signed up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aad293e3b2a4ca071affa395396afe4bd71a9ae6202b3040e3761e240464603"} {"question" "q-353261" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"} {"question" "q-353261" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "393"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063765/state.json" "file_sha256" "91af60b4dfa4beaddcad55d8f7a75e89d29d216d8dc7784a986501182b26c1b8"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2067 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
